From e8e7421c4f28976204f8307c8ac58d930d3b925e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Steven Huang <shuang@myai001.mylab.io>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 23:43:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] chore(dist): decompose one pipeline handoff on the host

A py-spy profile attributed a quarter of the bs=1 decode step to
recv_tensor_dict but said nothing about which part of it. Whether the cost is
the pickled metadata round trip, the per-tensor blocking receive, or the
all-gather decides what is worth changing, and guessing had already produced one
wrong conclusion today.

SGLANG_OPT_PP_HANDOFF_DIAG=1 logs a per-phase breakdown, once per
SGLANG_OPT_PP_HANDOFF_EVERY calls (default 200), with the payload it carries.

Host-side timing only. Nothing here synchronises with the device, which is the
whole constraint: this runs inside captured CUDA graphs, so one added .item()
would break capture. When the flag is off the only added cost is a call that
returns False.

The answer was that it is worth almost nothing. Steady state at bs=1:

  RECV metadata=0.014ms irecv=0.000ms allgather=0.000ms total=0.015ms

Three hops is 0.045 ms against a 37.3 ms step -- 0.1%. The payload is one
(6, 16384) fp16 tensor and 177 bytes of pickle. The path is not the bottleneck
and rewriting it would buy nothing.

What the measurement does show is where the cost went instead: the profile's
time is in _pp_commit_comm_work, which waits on the async sends issued earlier.
Those are fast to issue and slow to complete, because at bs=1 the peer is still
computing the previous token. The wait absorbs the pipeline stall rather than
being overhead of its own.

