From a588149ab0bc1c4a91dc8674b6ca6a8f28d9b127 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Steven Huang <shuang@myai001.mylab.io>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 23:16:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] fix(function_call): recover drifted DeepSeek tool calls
 instead of dropping them
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An agent loop against a live server died in two different ways, and both were
the parser's fault rather than the model's. A 45-message session made 21 tool
calls; the failures clustered here:

  1. A parameter written without its string attribute, or with the attribute
     value unquoted or capitalised. The attribute was mandatory in
     parameter_regex, so the parameter did not match at all, DSML text was left
     over, and the whole call was dropped as malformed.
  2. The same call, closed with a plain tag instead of a ｜DSML｜ prefixed one.
     Same cause: the closer could not match, so the leftover check tripped.
  3. The name written as `Bash` or `Read` where the client declared `bash` and
     `read`. The server passed the name through unchanged, the client answered
     "Tool Read not found", the model retried, and the session made no progress.

Closers are now matched with the ｜DSML｜ prefix optional, while open tags stay
strict so prose that quotes a tag is not mistaken for a call. The string
attribute is optional, and its value is read case-insensitively; when it is
absent or unrecognised the value is parsed as JSON and falls back to a plain
string, so an attribute slip cannot cost the call. Name repair moved into
BaseFormatDetector._canonical_tool_name: trim whitespace and quotes, drop an
OpenAI-style `functions.` prefix, then match case-insensitively against the
declared tools. Names that match no declared tool are returned as they came.

Case-insensitive matching belongs in both paths, which is how the third failure
survived the first round of fixes: the streaming path builds its ToolCallItem
directly and never goes through parse_base_json, so repairing the name there
only served one-shot clients. Streaming clients, which is what an agent loop
uses, kept receiving the raw name. Unknown names are still forwarded rather
than dropped, because a client error is what lets the model correct itself; the
streaming path now logs them, which it previously did not, so this class of
drift is no longer invisible in the server log.

Accepting an attribute-less parameter reverses one documented decision: that
test case asserted the call be dropped. It is replaced with tests for the
drift shapes that actually occur, and the bounded cases -- a self-closed
parameter, a parameter that never closes -- still fail closed.

The attribute and closer fixes are confirmed by a live 21-turn agent session
that logged no dropped call and no malformed drop. The name repair is the one
fix not yet seen in the field: the session that produced `Tool Read not found`
ran before it shipped, and the session after it happened to spell every name in
lowercase. It is therefore covered by tests at several chunk widths rather than
by a field log. test_deepseekv4_detector is 32 tests OK, up from 25. The other
function-call suites report exactly the baseline failures, unchanged:
test_deepseekv41_detector (4 errors) and test_function_call_parser (4 failures,
8 errors) were already red on this commit, and the two unknown-tool semantics
tests still pass.
